    "surface.title": "Surface & Flat Placements Explained",
    "surface.p1": "Why do surface piercings and dermal anchors carry substantially higher rejection rates than earlobes, septums, or lips? In a tissue fold, the jewellery passes through a natural crease or flap of flesh with two opposing skin walls. The forces acting on both ends are balanced, and skin movement does not pull perpendicular to the bar.",
    "surface.p2": "On a flat surface (such as the sternum, collarbone, nape, or wrist), skin is under continuous tension as muscles flex, skin stretches, and garments drag across it. There is no natural fold of tissue to cradle the post. The human immune and healing response naturally treats a rigid foreign object under flat skin as an intrusion to be expelled toward the surface: the path of least resistance.",
    "surface.p3": "Surface barbells use 90-degree stepped bends to anchor the shaft in the subcutaneous layer, while single-point dermal anchors rely on a perforated footplate that tissue weaves through. If the initial pocket was pierced too shallowly, or if frequent snagging levers the footplate, the anchor tilts and irreversible rejection begins.",
    "piercer.title": "What a Piercer Can Actually Do",
    "piercer.intro": "A piercer has practical options to manage early signs of trouble, but they cannot regenerate tissue that has already thinned. Here is what they evaluate:",
    "piercer.downsize_title": "1. Downsize to a Shorter Post",
    "piercer.downsize_desc": "Once swelling subsides, an overly long post snags on everything and tilts. Switching to a snug, flush-fitting post removes the levering action that triggers migration.",
    "piercer.change_jewellery_title": "2. Switch Jewellery Style, Tops, or Material",
    "piercer.change_jewellery_desc": "Switching from heavy or high-profile decorative gems to ultra-low-profile flat disks minimizes clothing drag. In flexible anatomies, biocompatible flexible posts reduce shear forces.",
    "piercer.resite_title": "3. Remove and Resite (Repierce Later)",
    "piercer.resite_desc": "If an angle was placed poorly, or if the tissue bite was insufficient, no amount of aftercare will keep it in place. A piercer can remove it cleanly, allow the area to heal and soften, and pierce it again at a deeper, more stable angle.",
    "piercer.honest_title": "4. The Honest Reality: An Actively Rejecting Piercing Usually Comes Out",
    "piercer.honest_desc": "Once the body has pushed a barbell past the halfway mark of the tissue bridge, it will almost never reverse direction or regenerate lost skin over the bar. Having a piercer remove the jewellery early leaves a tiny, faint dot instead of a split scar, torn skin, or deep cleft.",
